//Section sort: find the minimum element and put at start index 
// | Case                 | Time Complexity |
// | -------------------- | --------------: |
// | **Best case**        |       **O(n²)** |
// | **Average case**     |       **O(n²)** |
// | **Worst case**       |       **O(n²)** |
// | **Space complexity** |        **O(1)** |


// When to use Selection Sort?

// Selection Sort can be useful when:

// The dataset is small
// Memory usage needs to be minimal → O(1) extra space
// You want to minimize the number of swaps — standard Selection Sort performs at most n - 1 swaps
// You're learning/implementing basic sorting algorithms
// When not to use it?

// For large datasets, Selection Sort is generally inefficient because it always takes O(n²) time. 
// More efficient algorithms such as Merge Sort, Quick Sort, or Heap Sort are typically preferred.



const arr = [4,3,2,1]

for(let i=0;i<arr.length;i++){
    let minIndex = i
    for(let j=i+1; j<arr.length;j++){
        if(arr[j]<arr[minIndex]){
            minIndex=j
        }
    }
    [arr[i], arr[minIndex]] = [arr[minIndex],arr[i]] 
}

